  "summary": "I added a \"Lights off\" button below the canvas. Clicking it switches to a dim night look and relabels it \"Lights on\"; clicking again restores the warm lighting. Typecheck passes. I rendered the night look once, with the default temporarily flipped to lights off: the dial, hands and pendulum stayed readable in a dim warm pool. I then raised the night ambient light from 0.5 to 0.9 so the room isn't pure black, and I did not re-render after that change. I did not click the button in a browser; the lights-on frame was only checked before the night-lighting rewiring, and the button's click handler wasn't exercised.\n\n- **How it switches:** the four warm lights from the bridge (`clockLights()`) now have runtime ids and are hidden when lights go off. A night set is shown instead: a cool dim ambient light, a faint warm light on the dial, and a faint glow inside the case. The changes are in `src/three-bridge/clock.scene.ts` and `src/main.ts`.\n- **State:** `window.__TASK_STATE__` is now exactly `{ lightsOn, pendulumAngleDeg }`, updated every frame. `pendulumAngleDeg` is the signed angle from vertical in degrees, with a peak of ±11°. I removed the earlier time and weight fields from that object."
